      ":question/worked-solution": "An equilibrium is a constant function $y(x)=c$. Set $dy/dx=0$ and test the resulting equation.\n\nFirst keep the original domain: $y>0$.\n\nRearranging once, test whether $f(c)=0$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}f(c)= \\\\ c^{2} \\ln{\\left(c \\right)} - e^{2}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\n- For $c=1$: Use $\\ln{\\left(1 \\right)}=0$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) &=- e^{2} + 1 \\ln{\\left(1 \\right)} \\\\ &=- e^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is nonzero, so it is not an equilibrium solution.\n\n- For $c=2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) &=- e^{2} + 4 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is nonzero, so it is not an equilibrium solution.\n\n- For $c=e$: Use $\\ln{\\left(e \\right)}=1$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}f(e)= \\\\ - e^{2} + e^{2} \\ln{\\left(e \\right)} \\\\ =0\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nThis is zero, so it is an equilibrium solution.\n\nSelect $y=e$.",
